{
  "rules": [
    "Downtrend bias: when the recent 30d history is falling (lower lows), the model forecast is systematically too high vs realized, especially from ~day 10 onward. Apply a downward level shift that grows with horizon (small at 1–5d, larger at 15–30d).",
    "Over-optimistic rebounds: the model frequently predicts mid-horizon recoveries/plateaus (often rising into the mid/upper 70s) that do not materialize; realized keeps drifting lower. Damp any forecasted rebound following a selloff by scaling positive slopes down strongly (e.g., cut rebound magnitude by ~50–80%).",
    "Overshoot after spikes: after high-price regimes (history in low/mid-80s) followed by a drop, the model mean-reverts upward too quickly; true path mean-reverts downward more and stays depressed. Enforce slower/partial mean reversion upward after a regime break.",
    "Horizon-dependent widening error: short-horizon (1–5d) errors are modest, but error variance and positive bias increase with horizon (10–30d). Use horizon-weighted shrinkage toward a lower trend/anchor (e.g., recent 5–10d average minus a trend term).",
    "Lag in trend recognition: the model reacts late to persistent declines (keeps forecasting near prior levels). Add a momentum/trend adjustment: if last 10–15d return is negative, subtract an extra term proportional to recent slope from all future steps.",
    "Cap upper tails in bearish regimes: when recent prices are below their 20–30d moving average and momentum is negative, cap forecast highs (prevent forecasts from exceeding a bearish-regime ceiling, e.g., MA20 or MA10 + small premium)."
  ]
}
